 ## Subtopics
 
-### Proposer Architectures
-*   **Transformer Proposer:** Uses a transformer encoder for input features and a decoder with learned queries for cross-attention to produce proposals. Best for capturing complex dependencies between coarse samples.
-*   **Pool Proposer:** Sums point features with positional encodings, passes through FC+ReLU, averages to a ray representation, then decodes via FC. Simpler and faster, suitable for less complex scenes.
-*   **MLPMix Proposer:** Applies a single MLPMix block to sorted features, averages, then decodes via FC. Balances complexity and performance.
-*   *When to choose:* Transformer for highest quality if compute allows; Pool/MLPMix for efficiency.
+### Transformer Proposer
+Uses a transformer encoder to process input features and a transformer decoder with $N_f$ learned queries to produce proposals via cross-attention. Chosen when high accuracy in feature interaction is needed [S1, unofficial].
+
+### Pool Proposer
+Sums point features with positional encodings, passes through an FC layer and ReLU, averages to form a ray representation, then decodes via an FC. Chosen for lower computational overhead compared to Transformers [S1, unofficial].
+
+### MLPMix Proposer
+Applies a single MLPMix block to stacked point features (sorted by position), followed by average pooling and an FC decoder. Chosen as a middle-ground between Pool and Transformer complexity [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Dominated by the coarse and fine network queries. The proposer adds negligible overhead due to its small size [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Similar to standard NeRF, plus storage for proposer parameters and intermediate features [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Performance:** Achieves superior view synthesis quality over NeRF and state-of-the-art on synthetic Blender benchmark and on par or better on real LLFF-NeRF scenes [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Scaling:** Can achieve 25% saving in computation by filtering out least promising samples based on predicted importance, without significantly sacrificing quality [S1, unofficial].
-*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiable sampling, improved quality, potential for adaptive computation.
-*   **Limitations:** Requires two-stage training; proposer architecture choice affects performance; potential instability if not pre-trained properly [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Time Complexity:** Adds the computational cost of the proposer network to the standard NeRF rendering time. The proposer is designed to be small relative to coarse/fine networks to have negligible overhead [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing coarse features for all $N_c$ samples to pass to the proposer [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Performance:** Achieves superior view synthesis quality over NeRF and state-of-the-art on synthetic Blender benchmarks and comparable performance on real LLFF-NeRF scenes [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** End-to-end differentiability allows learning optimal sampling strategies; can achieve 25% computation savings by filtering low-importance samples without quality loss [S1, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** Training from scratch is unstable; requires a two-stage training strategy [S1, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-NeRF-ID was introduced in the paper "NeRF in detail: Learning to sample for view synthesis" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of vanilla NeRF's coarse-to-fine sampling, which is heuristic and not trained end-to-end. The method was developed to improve view synthesis quality by learning the sampling distribution directly [S1, unofficial].
+NeRF-ID was introduced in the paper "NeRF in detail: Learning to sample for view synthesis" [S1, unofficial]. It addresses the limitation of vanilla NeRF's coarse-to-fine sampling, which relies on a heuristic piece-wise constant PDF computed from coarse densities [S1, unofficial]. The method was developed to enable end-to-end training of the sampling mechanism, improving view synthesis quality [S1, unofficial].
 
